and products, motorcycles, TVs, and things like that

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:50.000
And we haven't really completely caught up

00:05:50.000 --> 00:06:03.000
But the main story here, though, is even though it did come back in manufacturing in the US adopted the total quality management principles that had developed

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:04.000
From this

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:10.000
Rebuilding of the Japanese manufacturing capability

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:15.000
And the real transition into information quality came

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:19.000
mostly through MIT

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:28.000
And a lot of the work of Richard Wong and also from the consulting work done by Larry English, who started

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:30.000
A company that

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:38.000
As I mentioned, was the real exposure to the problems of quality and information

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:42.000
came with the data warehousing movement

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:53.000
And so there were a lot of people then that caught on to this idea that you could adapt the manufacturing quality to building information

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:56.000
products and services

00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:04.000
And so now we have a lot of people that have emerged from that. Again, Dr. Wang

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:11.000
Larry English, who's a pioneer in that. Tom Redmond. Larry, of course, passed away a few years ago, but

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:20.000
Richard and Tom Redmond, Danette McGilvery, Laura Sebastian Coleman, Dave Loch, and some others

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:26.000
very notable in this area and they're still practicing

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:30.000
So we'll talk about each of those and their contribution.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:36.000
And as I was saying, we're beginning to enter into a new age

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:38.000
With AI

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:48.000
And actually, again, Rich and MIT and others are sort of leading the way in what we call Agentic data quality using

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:52.000
AI agents to help with

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:55.000
improve the quality of data

00:07:55.000 --> 00:08:00.000
And so we'll be doing some of that in our class

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:05.000
So let's go back a ways. So Walter Shuhart

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:10.000
He was American physicist, engineer, and statistician

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:21.000
And his big contribution, which is still very important, he's the father of what's called statistical process control

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:27.000
And the idea that you need to constantly look at what you're producing

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:35.000
So while we're on this, let me point out to you, when we're talking about quality, there's a couple of things that

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:40.000
We won't learn all about quality, but

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:43.000
A couple of things. One is

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:48.000
To have any process that you consider to be a quality process

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:56.000
then you… you need to recognize some things. One is, and I think we mentioned this, or I mentioned this before

00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:05.000
Is the difference between quality assurance and quality control, and we'll see that as we talk about some of these people

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:13.000
But it all comes around to this idea that what we're really doing is building information products and services

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:16.000
So, in the manufacturing process

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:32.000
quality control is the check against the product that you built. If you're building a car, it would be quality control comes at the point that you've actually completely manufactured the car and you're ready to ship it

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:38.000
So before it shipped, you have certain standards, you have certain requirements

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:46.000
that that product or that service has to meet and if it meets those, then it passes quality control

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:53.000
And I think I also mentioned earlier that we've kind of gone through some cycles here in the United States

00:09:53.000 --> 00:10:01.000
between quality control, which is inspecting the final product

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:04.000
And then the other side of that is quality assurance

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:11.000
And quality assurance are those things that you do during the building process, during the manufacturing process.

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:15.000
To check to be sure that

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:22.000
that the final product or the final service that you build will meet quality control standards

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:33.000
And those things that you do during the process are called quality assurance. You're trying to assure that you will meet quality control

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:36.000
So that's a couple of things. The other is that

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:41.000
All of the failures of quality assurance and quality control

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:43.000
are recorded

00:10:43.000 --> 00:11:00.000
or should be recorded, and then those should be analyzed to try to continually improve your process to eliminate those sources of problems that you detect through quality assurance and quality control. So those are 2 very essential parts of

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:05.000
Quality process. So anyway, back to

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:08.000
Shuhart

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:17.000
His idea was that you need to constantly check your product, your quality control

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:19.000
And so

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:24.000
It turns out then that it depends on what you're building, right?

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:32.000
So in many cases, you can't inspect every product that you build

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:38.000
For a car, you probably can. I mean, even though they built hundreds of cars a day

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:41.000
It's practical to go in and

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:47.000
inspect every one of them to be sure that it meets the quality control standards that you have

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:51.000
But for other things

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:55.000
can prove to be very difficult or in some cases impossible

00:11:55.000 --> 00:12:00.000
So in the case, for example, suppose you're building

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:03.000
Hairpins

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:08.000
And you're going to produce millions of these hairpins, or hundreds of thousands of these a day

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:14.000
But you want to be sure they're good, they're correct, and they're doing what they're supposed to do.

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:27.000
And so, in that case, what you would do is you would sample those. You couldn't… you couldn't expect every one of them. So you would have a way of sampling the outputs, all the hairpins you were building

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:29.000
And

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:39.000
And then you would base it on that. And that's what statistical process control. We call it statistical. We do sampling

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:45.000
The other place where it'd be impossible if you're building bonbons or some kind of candy

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:51.000
You're manufacturing some kind of food, you wouldn't be able to

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:55.000
taste every one of them, or you wouldn't have anything to sell, right?

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:57.000
So

00:12:57.000 --> 00:13:01.000
So anyway, this is the reason then for statistical process control

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:09.000
And he pioneered the application of statistics like sampling and other things to the idea of quality

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:11.000
And

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:19.000
And some of the principles he establishes that data have no meaning apart from their context. So it only becomes information

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:23.000
When data is put into the proper context

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:30.000
The other thing, and this became quite important, especially for some others that we'll talk about

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:37.000
The data contain both signals and noise, and so this is the beginning of this idea that you can have

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:44.000
Poor quality data. There's things in the data that you don't want, something that's wrong, anomalies

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:54.000
And here, he was again looking at it from a physicist standpoint, and he was saying, well, it's signal and noise. In other words, it's good data and

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:56.000
Problem data

00:13:56.000 --> 00:14:05.000
And you have to separate those, and so this is sort of the beginning of the idea of data quality control

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:11.000
And then this is something interesting because this gets misattributed a lot

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:18.000
He invented the idea of plan, do, check, act, cycle, this repeated cycle, PDCA

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:31.000
Unfortunately, most people believe that dimming that we'll talk about later, invented this, but he didn't. It was Shuhart, and he introduced it to Dimming. Deming and Schuhart worked together quite a bit

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:38.000
So the PDCA cycle really belongs to or was invented by Schuhart

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:46.000
So again, statistical process control is a very simplified thing. We'll talk more about it later

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:55.000
is that it's a time thing, right? When you're building something and manufacturing something, you're repeatedly building it over time

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:57.000
And you have

00:14:57.000 --> 00:15:02.000
You're going to be sampling and looking for things that

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:19.000
out of range or out of order, anomalies that you don't want. And so the idea is that over time you see time moving from left to right in this chart. You take a sample and you look and see if there are defects

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:21.000
And so

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:31.000
there's certain what we call tolerance, right? You can't stop the production line just because some things are defective. You want to try to remove them, but

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:34.000
You're looking for defects

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:39.000
And these this graph represents things

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:50.000
The samples have certain levels of defects in them, but if they stay within what we call the control limits, upper and lower control limits

00:15:50.000 --> 00:16:03.000
then it makes sense and you look at that and you say, well, yeah, there's going to be some natural variation, some normal variation

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:05.000
in any manufacturing process

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:13.000
machines are going to wear and they're going to make some mistakes

00:16:13.000 --> 00:16:18.000
As they… as the machines get older, or you have different operators and so forth

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:27.000
But as long as you stay within the limits, so what you're trying to look for in statistical process control is when a process goes out of control

00:16:27.000 --> 00:16:37.000
When you start seeing continual products or anomalies and they start exceeding your limits, either your lower limits or your upper limits

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:42.000
And so this is the idea of statistical process control

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:45.000
And that's Schuhart's big contribution.

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:47.000
And

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:57.000
So again, he worked closely with Edward Deming, who's perhaps one of the more famous people in the area of quality, especially in manufacturing.

00:16:57.000 --> 00:17:05.000
And again, he was a statistician, but he was also a professor and an engineer as well.

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:13.000
He's a lot more philosophical than Schuhart. He came up with what he called a system of profound knowledge

00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:18.000
And he was a big

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:29.000
on the list, he came up with 14 points for organizational transformation how to transform organizations. So in some ways, not only was he father of

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:31.000
quality

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:48.000
But this is also sort of the beginning of what we call change management, which is another course that we'll be offering in the spring that talks about how you transform organizations to stay current

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:58.000
And then perhaps the thing that he's most famous for is the seven deadly sins of management. We'll talk a little bit about those

00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:07.000
The seven deadly sins is one of his big contributions. Now he adopted Shuhart's plan, do, check, act

00:18:07.000 --> 00:18:11.000
But he felt like that instead of checking

00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:25.000
of what had happened. If you find something wrong, you should study it. So he changed the plan, do, check act into plan do study act, PDSA, just

00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:29.000
make it his own, that's what consultants do

00:18:29.000 --> 00:18:37.000
And he was a big advocate and promoter of Schuhart's statistical process control

00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:40.000
And

00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:42.000
Then again

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:48.000
The importance of process thinking, thinking about things in terms of the process that's used to

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:50.000
Create something

00:18:50.000 --> 00:19:02.000
So this is some of the important points of dimming, and again quite famous. There's several, there's still a dimming society, and they still give awards for

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:06.000
Good quality management

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:12.000
And sort of the key message that Deming has is that

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:16.000
He was coming from a time in manufacturing

00:19:16.000 --> 00:19:20.000
We're manufacturers, if there were problems with their products

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:26.000
always tended to blame the workers. Oh, you know, somebody messed up on the assembly line, or whatever

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:34.000
And Deming's message was that they had it all backwards, that these problems and the cause of these problems

00:19:34.000 --> 00:19:46.000
We're mostly in the management of the process. The management was 90% responsible and only 10% of the problems were actually coming from the workers. So he really turned that on its head

00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:52.000
And he came up with the seven deadly sins. Again, probably

00:19:52.000 --> 00:19:58.000
His most famous contribution to quality

00:19:58.000 --> 00:20:03.000
And so the first one is the lack of constancy of purpose

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:13.000
In other words, the idea that when you're making something, you're manufacturing, you keep changing your target, you keep moving and

00:20:13.000 --> 00:20:18.000
It's certainly true that you need to manage the life cycle of a product and not

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:34.000
keep making the same thing over and over, even when it's not popular or selling. But at the same time, you can't just keep changing the direction that you're going constantly, because this really causes

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:50.000
The workers to lose attention and become confused about what's the real mission that you're under. So when you see these sorts of changes, he says that's a symptom of a problem that's going to result in poor quality

00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:56.000
And this one, I wish people would still

00:20:56.000 --> 00:20:58.000
think about this one

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:02.000
And that emphasis on short-term profits

00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:08.000
And that's certainly a huge problem that we have today because now in our current financial situation.

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:10.000
The way

00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:17.000
The observers of work is that now everything is on a quarterly basis, right?

00:21:17.000 --> 00:21:22.000
Instead of thinking about the year, two years, things that ahead

00:21:22.000 --> 00:21:25.000
And what's going to be the long-term benefit

00:21:25.000 --> 00:21:32.000
The financial analysts are always on everybody's case at the end of a quarter

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:41.000
And they try to project what you should be making, what kind of profits you have each quarter, rather than thinking of the long-term investment.

00:21:41.000 --> 00:21:45.000
But anyway, he said that was a symptom of poor quality

00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:53.000
constantly looking at short-term, somewhat like constancy of purpose, where you're changing your focus

00:21:53.000 --> 00:21:57.000
on short term

00:21:57.000 --> 00:22:07.000
Another is performance appraisals that promote fear. And again, this is still a bit of a problem, I think, in modern

00:22:07.000 --> 00:22:11.000
corporations, but not as bad as it was in the old days

00:22:11.000 --> 00:22:14.000
And this is the idea that

00:22:14.000 --> 00:22:25.000
you know, we're watching you and your performance and you have to meet certain standards or quotas or you know you're going to get fired, you're going to get demoted

00:22:25.000 --> 00:22:34.000
Instead of trying to encourage workers, you tried to kind of motivate them by fear of losing their job.

00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:40.000
The other, and again, some of these still

00:22:40.000 --> 00:22:43.000
are important today

00:22:43.000 --> 00:22:46.000
This idea of mobility of management

00:22:46.000 --> 00:22:51.000
And we still sort of have this idea

00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:56.000
For example, now, even here at the university, we confer a degree

00:22:56.000 --> 00:22:58.000
a degree in management

00:22:58.000 --> 00:23:02.000
And it's the idea that once you learn the principles of management

00:23:02.000 --> 00:23:05.000
You can sort of manage anything

00:23:05.000 --> 00:23:11.000
And so what he was arguing against here in his day in the manufacturing processes

00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:15.000
They often had this idea of rotating management

00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:18.000
And so the guy who was

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:22.000
Managing the painting of the product

00:23:22.000 --> 00:23:36.000
would periodically shift, and now he would be managing inventory or some other part of the company. The idea that you, you know, management is management, and you didn't have to learn the particulars in a particular context

00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:38.000
He said that was a problem

00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:45.000
And then management by the use of visible numbers, or quotas

00:23:45.000 --> 00:23:51.000
And this this is again, we see this a lot today

00:23:51.000 --> 00:24:01.000
The idea that, you know, you're judging your employees by certain quotas of what they're able to do. This happens not only in manufacturing, but in sales and many other things.

00:24:01.000 --> 00:24:06.000
And also, it's very important to note, he said it's by visible numbers

00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:12.000
You know, I've seen, you know, like when they do fundraising and they put a big thermometer on the wall

00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:17.000
So, you know, we've reached this level of contribution, or that level

00:24:17.000 --> 00:24:19.000
and making it visible to everyone.

00:24:19.000 --> 00:24:23.000
He said that's not a good way to manage.

00:24:23.000 --> 00:24:29.000
Also, if you have and again, this has to do mostly with the manufacturing where people worked on a shop floor

00:24:29.000 --> 00:24:43.000
that excessive medical costs, that's an indicator that things not only were not saved but that the processes were not being run properly, and that could result in poor quality

00:24:43.000 --> 00:24:49.000
And also excessive cost of liability. If you have liability

00:24:49.000 --> 00:24:53.000
producing something that causes problems

00:24:53.000 --> 00:24:57.000
We see this all the time in recalls, right?

00:24:57.000 --> 00:25:03.000
Something is manufactured and it turns out to be… have some unforeseen

00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:22.000
problem when it gets out into the public and they start using it. So excessive cost of liability. So he said these are the main things that you should look for to see if companies were really functioning and producing quality products or not

00:25:22.000 --> 00:25:30.000
So, and again, the big issue here is this idea of continuous improvement

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:36.000
So again, if you look back at the Plan Do Check Act, it is a

00:25:36.000 --> 00:25:42.000
A cycle. It's not something that just happens once. It's a cycle

00:25:42.000 --> 00:25:49.000
So, you plan to do something, you do it, and then you see if you actually were able to do it correctly

00:25:49.000 --> 00:26:03.000
And if you weren't able to do that, then you act on that to improve it and improve your plan and do it differently so that this results in a continuous improvement.

00:26:03.000 --> 00:26:09.000
So Dilling had a lot of contribution, again, still very considered very important today.

00:26:09.000 --> 00:26:16.000
And by the way, again, the Japanese contributed a lot to this

00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:29.000
And we still use today a lot of words from Japan because they were so enthusiastic about adopting these things and they had their own, of course, language for describing them

00:26:29.000 --> 00:26:35.000
And so this idea of continuous improvement is called Kaizen

00:26:35.000 --> 00:26:44.000
Which in Japanese means change for the better. In other words, you keep making it better

00:26:44.000 --> 00:26:54.000
Another very important pioneer of quality was a guy named Joseph Duran

00:26:54.000 --> 00:26:59.000
And he's most famous for what's called the Quality Trilogy

00:26:59.000 --> 00:27:04.000
Now, it's interesting that it's sort of a

00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:08.000
The plan to check act without one of the

00:27:08.000 --> 00:27:10.000
The

00:27:10.000 --> 00:27:13.000
For part, because it's a trilogy

00:27:13.000 --> 00:27:20.000
And but what he's famous for is he says that quality comes

00:27:20.000 --> 00:27:24.000
From these three things. It comes from planning

00:27:24.000 --> 00:27:25.000
improvement

00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:37.000
Which sort of automatically includes the idea of study. You can't really improve something if you don't study and understand what you need to improve

00:27:37.000 --> 00:27:39.000
And then control

00:27:39.000 --> 00:27:41.000
And so

00:27:41.000 --> 00:27:49.000
He's very famous for this idea of quality control, and he made the statement

00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:55.000
that you can tell quality

00:27:55.000 --> 00:28:02.000
If you build something that is fit for purpose or fit for use

00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:12.000
And so he's sort of the source of some people use this definition and they say, well, what is data quality? They say, well, it's data that's fit for use

00:28:12.000 --> 00:28:16.000
And this comes really from Duran in one of his books

00:28:16.000 --> 00:28:22.000
But again, as we said earlier in the course

00:28:22.000 --> 00:28:29.000
We tend to separate the concepts of data quality and information quality

00:28:29.000 --> 00:28:34.000
Certainly, data quality is meeting data requirements

00:28:34.000 --> 00:28:48.000
That's the definition of falsity, and ISO is quality is meeting requirements. So, data quality is meeting data requirements, but just because data meets the requirements, you've got good data, as they say

00:28:48.000 --> 00:28:57.000
You put it into a process doesn't mean that you will get a quality product out of it. You'll get a product and quality information product

00:28:57.000 --> 00:29:15.000
There has to be a process, and it has to be planned. You have to continually improve that process, and you're really focusing on the quality control. That is, after you build it, is the thing you built have quality? Does it meet requirements

00:29:15.000 --> 00:29:17.000
of the users of that.

00:29:17.000 --> 00:29:30.000
So you're looking at both ends of this process. In the raw product ingestion, when you're bringing data in, you want data to be good, you want it to be clean, you want it to meet requirements

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:39.000
But you still have to check at the end and manage it as a product. In other words, you're taking that data, you're putting it through processes

00:29:39.000 --> 00:29:47.000
And you're coming out with an information product or service, then you have to check that as well.

00:29:47.000 --> 00:29:52.000
And so there's an but they're different kinds of requirements, right? They're not requirements like

00:29:52.000 --> 00:29:55.000
coverage and

00:29:55.000 --> 00:30:11.000
consistency and those sorts of things. Now you're talking about the requirements of the user. Is that… is it really what they want? Is it doing what they want it to do? So you have different types of requirements. So it's still quality in the sense of meeting requirements

00:30:11.000 --> 00:30:14.000
But they're different kinds of requirements.

00:30:14.000 --> 00:30:27.000
On the data quality side, on the left-hand side, as data comes in, the requirements have to do with characteristics of the data. The amount of data, the manipulability of the data.

00:30:27.000 --> 00:30:33.000
The relevance of the data, the accuracy of the data

00:30:33.000 --> 00:30:38.000
And you're looking at that data in terms of those data quality dimensions

00:30:38.000 --> 00:30:54.000
But then when you run it through the process and you produce an information product or service, you have different kinds of requirements there. Those are the value requirements. How valuable is what you've built in terms of is it producing value for the organization

00:30:54.000 --> 00:30:59.000
And is it producing value for the users of that product or service?

00:30:59.000 --> 00:31:10.000
And so then it becomes more of a value proposition. So we'll continue to talk about this idea

00:31:10.000 --> 00:31:12.000
Okay, so

00:31:12.000 --> 00:31:18.000
He was also big on quality planning. You needed to ask these W questions. So

00:31:18.000 --> 00:31:20.000
who

00:31:20.000 --> 00:31:25.000
What and how? Who are your customers

00:31:25.000 --> 00:31:31.000
What do they want? What are their quality characteristics

00:31:31.000 --> 00:31:41.000
So their quality characteristics may not be the ones that we think of when we're ingesting data and using data quality requirements

00:31:41.000 --> 00:31:58.000
Their characteristics, they're buying a car, they want to know how does it handle? Does it run smoothly? Is the interior quiet? All kinds of things that are not part of the parts that go into

00:31:58.000 --> 00:32:07.000
The building of the car, I mean, certainly there's a connection there, but there are different types of requirements when you're building the car

00:32:07.000 --> 00:32:19.000
You're interested in the parts fit together, the tolerances in terms of the diameters and the tensile strength of the parts, and so forth, you're looking at those intrinsic qualities

00:32:19.000 --> 00:32:28.000
But after you build the car, build that product, then it's a different set of requirements, but those are the requirements of the consumer of that product.

00:32:28.000 --> 00:32:31.000
And the same thing is true for data

00:32:31.000 --> 00:32:49.000
So you're building an information product like we talked earlier, you're building here at the school, they'll build your transcript right or they'll build your bill, your fees and those sort of thing. They have put together all this information

00:32:49.000 --> 00:32:54.000
to build that product that's useful and for you

00:32:54.000 --> 00:32:57.000
And then how

00:32:57.000 --> 00:33:08.000
How do you make it, how you'll deliver it, all the things that go into the product service itself.

00:33:08.000 --> 00:33:16.000
So again, this is sort of his contribution, but again, I see this as one of the places where

00:33:16.000 --> 00:33:27.000
People have kind of misapplied what Joseph… what Duran was saying when they say, you know, well, what is data quality? Well, it's data that's fit for use

00:33:27.000 --> 00:33:37.000
And that's, it's okay, but what he was talking about was the product, not the data, or not the parts that go in

00:33:37.000 --> 00:33:51.000
He was talking about quality control. He was saying, you know, that quality is when you build something that people want and use and it fits their, it fits their purpose of what they want to use it for

00:33:51.000 --> 00:34:09.000
If it's a card, it pulls a trailer, it moves dirt, or it's quiet, or it saves gas, or it's powerful. Those are the kinds of things, not the kinds of things we think about when we think about

00:34:09.000 --> 00:34:19.000
The input side, where we're inputting the materials and the parts. So there's different quality characteristics that we're looking for

00:34:19.000 --> 00:34:23.000
But nevertheless, I see that all the time still

00:34:23.000 --> 00:34:34.000
So his big emphasis was on quality control. Again, and quality control is at the end, after you've built what you're building, whether it's a data product

00:34:34.000 --> 00:34:49.000
I'm sorry, an information product or a manufactured product. Quality control is checking that the final product is meeting your requirements of the user

00:34:49.000 --> 00:34:54.000
So in his idea was that this is the way you should start

00:34:54.000 --> 00:34:56.000
And again, this

00:34:56.000 --> 00:35:02.000
I don't know why when we moved all of these principles over

00:35:02.000 --> 00:35:03.000
to

00:35:03.000 --> 00:35:15.000
To information quality, it took us so long to kind of realize what he was talking about here. And that is, the way you manage information or manage information

00:35:15.000 --> 00:35:29.000
is as a product. You need to really think of it as a product, and this is exactly what he's saying here. Quality control, in Duran's view, is the governance process that makes sure things don't get worse

00:35:29.000 --> 00:35:42.000
In other words, you really need to focus on the end product that you're building, whether it's an information product or a manufactured product. That's what tells you whether or not you're on the right track or not

00:35:42.000 --> 00:35:54.000
And of course, that's inspection of actual performance against plan performance the way you intended the product to work or the information product to do

00:35:54.000 --> 00:35:57.000
Is it really meeting that

00:35:57.000 --> 00:36:07.000
And of course, if there are differences, then that calls for an analysis we call a root cause investigation, a root cause analysis

00:36:07.000 --> 00:36:15.000
And then again, he's very much on the camp of Schuhart and Deming to say that

00:36:15.000 --> 00:36:26.000
You know, in many cases, you have to use statistical measures of performance to ensure control, because in many cases, it's impractical to inspect

00:36:26.000 --> 00:36:32.000
Each product individually. So often you have to come to some kind of

00:36:32.000 --> 00:36:34.000
sampling method

00:36:34.000 --> 00:36:41.000
And so quality improvement is focused on fitness for use and error reduction

00:36:41.000 --> 00:36:45.000
So again, he's talking about when he says fitness for use

00:36:45.000 --> 00:36:48.000
Our fitness for purpose

00:36:48.000 --> 00:36:56.000
the terms that people use. He's talking about the product itself

00:36:56.000 --> 00:37:02.000
And you want to keep improving that, this idea of continuous improvement.

00:37:02.000 --> 00:37:06.000
He also advocated a thing called breakdown thinking

00:37:06.000 --> 00:37:14.000
To change the system and improve performance. And what he meant by breakthrough thinking

00:37:14.000 --> 00:37:20.000
Again, he thought that even though you wanted continuous improvement

00:37:20.000 --> 00:37:26.000
He wanted people to think about what would be a radical change

00:37:26.000 --> 00:37:29.000
That can improve. What's a breakthrough thinking

00:37:29.000 --> 00:37:38.000
that could, you know, let's just think about how would we… could we completely change what we're doing to make things even better

00:37:38.000 --> 00:37:42.000
So, again, even though he was into this idea of

00:37:42.000 --> 00:37:46.000
Quality improvement and the idea that

00:37:46.000 --> 00:37:49.000
Deming and others had talked about continuous improvement

00:37:49.000 --> 00:37:55.000
It's sort of like now the modern view of evolution, you know, I mean

00:37:55.000 --> 00:38:00.000
Darwin's initial idea is that it was a very, very slow change over generations

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:11.000
But there's also introduced the same thing that there are certain things, there's certain radical quick changes

00:38:11.000 --> 00:38:15.000
For example, the asteroid that hit the Earth

00:38:15.000 --> 00:38:21.000
completely changed the evolution of dinosaurs and reptiles

00:38:21.000 --> 00:38:39.000
shifted over to mammals that you have to think about both. You have to think about continually improving the process that you have, but be open-minded to think, is there a new way, a big change in the process that could really make things better?

00:38:39.000 --> 00:38:42.000
So Duran had a lot to contribute

00:38:42.000 --> 00:38:46.000
And then we get into the more modern era

00:38:46.000 --> 00:38:52.000
And a guy named Armand Feigenbaum

00:38:52.000 --> 00:38:55.000
And by this time

00:38:55.000 --> 00:39:04.000
quality control and become a profession, and he was a quality control person. That was his occupation expert

00:39:04.000 --> 00:39:07.000
And

00:39:07.000 --> 00:39:17.000
is the one who's responsible for this term TQM or total quality control or total quality management

00:39:17.000 --> 00:39:19.000
So

00:39:19.000 --> 00:39:27.000
And the idea here, the total means that everyone in the process is thinking about

00:39:27.000 --> 00:39:34.000
The end product, and is it going to meet the consumer or the customer's need

00:39:34.000 --> 00:39:43.000
So even the people that are sitting in the plant, they're building a car, they need to be thinking about what they're doing as contributing

00:39:43.000 --> 00:39:54.000
to the end product, and that everyone is thinking, everyone in the process, they're not just thinking about putting this bolt on that they have to do 100 times every day

00:39:54.000 --> 00:40:04.000
Then what that contributes to in terms of the overall quality of the product, in terms of what customers want

00:40:04.000 --> 00:40:07.000
He also

00:40:07.000 --> 00:40:10.000
Came up with the idea

00:40:10.000 --> 00:40:16.000
Excuse me, what was called a hidden plan

00:40:16.000 --> 00:40:22.000
And I think all of you have seen this at one time or another. When you have some kind of process

00:40:22.000 --> 00:40:29.000
where you're building something, or you're building, or you're performing some kind of service

00:40:29.000 --> 00:40:35.000
that you have planned out a nice process that's supposed to work

00:40:35.000 --> 00:40:39.000
But when it starts in certain places where it's not working

00:40:39.000 --> 00:40:44.000
Instead of fixing that process at that point to make it work

00:40:44.000 --> 00:40:47.000
We work around it

00:40:47.000 --> 00:40:51.000
In other words, we get to, you know, we produce a report

00:40:51.000 --> 00:41:00.000
And that report should have controls on it so that we should just be able to take that report and deliver it to the user of that report

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:16.000
But we know that in the process of building that report, there are certain things that are just not working properly. It's not doing exactly what the people that are using the report want. So you have to stop and intercept the report, and instead of giving it directly to the user

00:41:16.000 --> 00:41:30.000
You have to say, okay, let's go in and check it. There's some things wrong here, so we have to go in and manually change some things and fix some things before we actually get the product that we want to deliver to the customer.

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:32.000
And so he calls this a hidden plant

00:41:32.000 --> 00:41:41.000
That this extra work or workarounds to correct mistakes that are within the ordinary factory

00:41:41.000 --> 00:41:50.000
And accountability for quality and the idea that quality, again, is part of the value proposition

00:41:50.000 --> 00:42:01.000
And the quality needs to be seen as valued, and it needs to be visible at the highest levels of management

00:42:01.000 --> 00:42:05.000
And then quality cost

00:42:05.000 --> 00:42:17.000
Now, this is we're going to see this is in sort of in contrast or maybe I shouldn't say contrast, but it's going to be sort of a different way of looking at this idea

00:42:17.000 --> 00:42:20.000
But Feigenbaum is saying

00:42:20.000 --> 00:42:22.000
That

00:42:22.000 --> 00:42:30.000
That we need to think about we need to realize that quality was going to cause it.

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:34.000
To have really quality product, we're going to have to do some things extra

00:42:34.000 --> 00:42:49.000
And you need to quantify the cost of the quality. Of course, you want it to be as small as possible, but it is going to cost more to get good quality, and I think most people recognize this. They're willing to pay more

00:42:49.000 --> 00:42:51.000
for products that are higher quality

00:42:51.000 --> 00:43:04.000
Because it costs more to produce them. But it's interesting, we'll see that there's another guy, a guy named Bill Crosby

00:43:04.000 --> 00:43:18.000
sort of looked at this in another way and again he is an American businessman. And here's what he said. He published a whole book that says, well, quality is free

00:43:18.000 --> 00:43:25.000
So this is in contrast to Feigenbaum, who said, well, you really need to pay attention and understand the cost

00:43:25.000 --> 00:43:31.000
And here is Crosby saying, well, but quality is free

00:43:31.000 --> 00:43:38.000
But again, they're really talking about the same thing. My example is that

00:43:38.000 --> 00:43:47.000
That certainly Crosby recognized it costs more to ensure quality took a little extra time, maybe it took extra workers

00:43:47.000 --> 00:43:51.000
Maybe it took more costly parts

00:43:51.000 --> 00:44:06.000
And information quality, it would be the time, like you're doing in your assignment of going through and looking at the data, seeing what the possible defects or anomalies might be, and then are they important

00:44:06.000 --> 00:44:14.000
Do we need to correct them? Do we need to take the time and effort to fix those things? So there's going to be extra cost

00:44:14.000 --> 00:44:21.000
But his point was, the reason he says that quality is free is that extra cost

00:44:21.000 --> 00:44:29.000
is more than compensated by the fact that the product that you're building is worth more

00:44:29.000 --> 00:44:43.000
People will… you'll sell more of it, or you can charge more for it, because it's a quality product. And so, in essence, by investing in the quality management part

00:44:43.000 --> 00:44:50.000
that you get that back and more, so that, in essence, the cost of the quality is free

00:44:50.000 --> 00:44:57.000
So again, it's just sort of another way of looking at Feigenbaum said. Certainly, there is cost to quality

00:44:57.000 --> 00:45:10.000
But Crosby's argument is that it really doesn't cost anything extra because you're going to more than make up for it in the price of the product or the

00:45:10.000 --> 00:45:14.000
the quantities that you sell of the product

00:45:14.000 --> 00:45:20.000
And this is not drift, it's the durst system that he invented

00:45:20.000 --> 00:45:28.000
doing it right the first time, right? So it's often said, you

00:45:28.000 --> 00:45:33.000
How… if you can all have time to do it now, how are you going to find the time to fix it later

00:45:33.000 --> 00:45:41.000
And again, quality, this is the ISO definition. I don't know if it really comes from him or not.

00:45:41.000 --> 00:45:49.000
It's the idea that quality is conformance or meeting requirements

00:45:49.000 --> 00:45:50.000
And

00:45:50.000 --> 00:46:02.000
The system of falsi, the essence of quality is prevention, right? So, just like you're doing and you're getting data in, you're going to create some kind of an information product, like a report card

00:46:02.000 --> 00:46:06.000
A grade transcript for

00:46:06.000 --> 00:46:10.000
A ride for transcript.

00:46:10.000 --> 00:46:17.000
You want to prevent errors as early in the process as possible

00:46:17.000 --> 00:46:26.000
And again, this is what I was talking about earlier. I don't remember if I told you

00:46:26.000 --> 00:46:33.000
Well, I will later

00:46:33.000 --> 00:46:38.000
the idea that… and you see this a lot even in software development

00:46:38.000 --> 00:46:41.000
Is this idea that it costs

00:46:41.000 --> 00:46:48.000
The cost to prevent problems is much smaller than the cost to repair things later, so

00:46:48.000 --> 00:46:53.000
whatever cost or time that it takes to do things

00:46:53.000 --> 00:46:59.000
correctly the first time is much less than what it takes to have to pull back

00:46:59.000 --> 00:47:07.000
product you see these recalls all the time of having to destroy a lot of food or recall a lot of

00:47:07.000 --> 00:47:11.000
product children's

00:47:11.000 --> 00:47:17.000
those seats for cars, car seats and things like that that have some kind of defect

00:47:17.000 --> 00:47:27.000
And then the cost becomes 10 hundred times more than the cost of just sort of, as he said, doing it right the first time

00:47:27.000 --> 00:47:37.000
And the performance standard, what we're all looking for is zero defects. However, this is one that's kind of fallen into

00:47:37.000 --> 00:47:41.000
I mean, if we look at Microsoft, right

00:47:41.000 --> 00:47:47.000
We want it to be as good as it needs to be. And certainly, it would be ideal

00:47:47.000 --> 00:47:56.000
If we had zero defects, but that can be impractical in many situations. It's just almost impossible in many cases

00:47:56.000 --> 00:48:00.000
to actually get rid of every error, every anomaly, every problem

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:04.000
And is it necessary

00:48:04.000 --> 00:48:11.000
You know, how much how many of these really prevent the thing that you're building from having

00:48:11.000 --> 00:48:17.000
Being useful and meeting the requirements of the consumer of that product

00:48:17.000 --> 00:48:22.000
But anyway, it's always good to think about it as a goal of zero

00:48:22.000 --> 00:48:27.000
But it's not always achievable

00:48:27.000 --> 00:48:36.000
And the measure of quality is the price of nonconformance. So again, this is where he gets the quality is free, right?

00:48:36.000 --> 00:48:41.000
If things don't conform and they cost you because the product is

00:48:41.000 --> 00:48:43.000
a meeting at

00:48:43.000 --> 00:48:48.000
The requirements of the consumer, then that tells you what

00:48:48.000 --> 00:48:58.000
What you should be able to spend, that's a cap, if you will, on how much you can invest in quality to try to eliminate the nonconformance.

00:48:58.000 --> 00:49:02.000
So again.

00:49:02.000 --> 00:49:05.000
He was very influential

00:49:05.000 --> 00:49:08.000
quality pioneer

00:49:08.000 --> 00:49:13.000
Alright, and then we see the shift over to Japan

00:49:13.000 --> 00:49:16.000
Were they were adopted

00:49:16.000 --> 00:49:23.000
Many of these principles of dimming and Schuhart and others before it came back to the Us.

00:49:23.000 --> 00:49:27.000
And so they're

00:49:27.000 --> 00:49:33.000
Again, you can see a lot of their words embedded into our current quality processes

00:49:33.000 --> 00:49:37.000
So Ishikawa

00:49:37.000 --> 00:49:41.000
Was a Japanese organizational theorist

00:49:41.000 --> 00:49:49.000
And he had this idea of user-friendly quality control. Again, quality control

00:49:49.000 --> 00:49:57.000
is the inspection or the check of conformance of the final product to

00:49:57.000 --> 00:49:59.000
Those

00:49:59.000 --> 00:50:10.000
The customer requirements, so what he means by user-friendly is he is the… the idea is that when, again, need to think about quality from the standpoint of

00:50:10.000 --> 00:50:18.000
the finished product that we're building, and is it friendly to the user? In other words, is it what the user is really wanting

00:50:18.000 --> 00:50:25.000
That's where we should really start, not with just, we're getting data, let's clean it up the best we can

00:50:25.000 --> 00:50:35.000
And this idea is really entrenched. People, even today, if you go out and talk to somebody about data quality or quality control or anything.

00:50:35.000 --> 00:50:41.000
They'll say, yeah, yeah, we get lots of data, and we spend a lot of time scrubbing and cleaning that data.

00:50:41.000 --> 00:50:45.000
Well, that's probably important

00:50:45.000 --> 00:50:51.000
But it's not where the focus should be. The focus should be on what am I building

00:50:51.000 --> 00:50:54.000
What is the product or service that I'm building

00:50:54.000 --> 00:51:11.000
From there, then I say, okay, what condition do I need the data to be in? First of all, what data do I need? And then what condition do I need it to be in, in order to build this product so that it meets the requirements of the people who are going to use it

00:51:11.000 --> 00:51:22.000
And not just say, well, we're getting data, let's clean it up as much as we can, and we'll throw it into the process, and surely everything will be okay.

00:51:22.000 --> 00:51:35.000
So it's sort of the misinterpretation or misquoting of garbage in, garbage out. That's certainly true. You put really bad data into a process, you're bound to get something bad out the other end.

00:51:35.000 --> 00:51:38.000
However, the converse is not true.

00:51:38.000 --> 00:51:46.000
The converse is that if I put in good data, I won't necessarily get a good product. That is not true

00:51:46.000 --> 00:51:47.000
It doesn't follow

00:51:47.000 --> 00:51:51.000
So you have to start with the product

00:51:51.000 --> 00:52:07.000
All you can say from garbage in, garbage out is that if you didn't get garbage out, in other words, you got something good out, all you can infer is, well, I didn't put bad data in because if I put the bad data in, I'd certainly have gotten bad product out

00:52:07.000 --> 00:52:12.000
So we have to be careful how we apply our lodging

00:52:12.000 --> 00:52:24.000
What he's most famous for is the Ishikawa diagram, which nobody probably knows what it is anymore. They always know it by the fishbone name

00:52:24.000 --> 00:52:30.000
So he was very much focused on the idea of root cause analysis

00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:39.000
That if something was going wrong at the product level, what do we trace it back to? What was causing that?

00:52:39.000 --> 00:52:45.000
And so he was very finalist for using these diagrams to sort of

00:52:45.000 --> 00:52:53.000
inspire or tickle the ideas of what are the possible things we should be looking at.

00:52:53.000 --> 00:53:06.000
He also, you may have heard of quality circles. This arose again in Japan. This idea of getting people together, sitting down in a circle or around a table, and talking about

00:53:06.000 --> 00:53:10.000
process and how people could

00:53:10.000 --> 00:53:21.000
get together and understand the entire process and maybe make better improvements to it, rather than just being focused on that part of the process that they were

00:53:21.000 --> 00:53:25.000
empowered with

00:53:25.000 --> 00:53:31.000
And then emphasize this idea of the internal customer as well, that the customers are not only

00:53:31.000 --> 00:53:34.000
The people that you're selling products to

00:53:34.000 --> 00:53:41.000
But there are many cases where your customer is internal. This is certainly true for information products

00:53:41.000 --> 00:53:44.000
So, here at the university.

00:53:44.000 --> 00:53:52.000
There's certain ones that go out. For example, I mentioned the fact that we have to report… we're getting ready to report our census data

00:53:52.000 --> 00:53:54.000
For this semester

00:53:54.000 --> 00:53:56.000
to the Department of Education

00:53:56.000 --> 00:53:59.000
This is an external customer

00:53:59.000 --> 00:54:04.000
But we have a lot of internal customers that are using data all the time

00:54:04.000 --> 00:54:09.000
an information products internal

00:54:09.000 --> 00:54:11.000
So we have to think about those

00:54:11.000 --> 00:54:15.000
A shared vision of what we're trying to accomplish

00:54:15.000 --> 00:54:19.000
And so here's the idea of the fishbone diagram

00:54:19.000 --> 00:54:23.000
And it's this idea that

00:54:23.000 --> 00:54:25.000
If you

00:54:25.000 --> 00:54:34.000
Look at the outcome, what you're trying, your product or your service that you're trying to build, and if there's a defect. So this was an example I found on the internet about

00:54:34.000 --> 00:54:37.000
The problem of being late for work. And so

00:54:37.000 --> 00:54:47.000
what could contribute to that? Why, you know, so it could be motivation, people are not motivated, they're tired, they're lazy, dreaming about vacations

00:54:47.000 --> 00:54:50.000
The method, they didn't set the alarm, they

00:54:50.000 --> 00:54:55.000
I don't have a regular time to wake

00:54:55.000 --> 00:54:57.000
route to work, time management

00:54:57.000 --> 00:55:08.000
kids, partners, family, things that could prevent us. So, it just goes on. This is just a very simple example. But the idea is, and there are many of these, depending on certain types of

00:55:08.000 --> 00:55:17.000
products and services that are being built that are already sort of pre-populated. And the whole idea is to get you to think, again.

00:55:17.000 --> 00:55:20.000
Back to

00:55:20.000 --> 00:55:26.000
This idea that we saw of breakthrough thinking, right

00:55:26.000 --> 00:55:30.000
You need to sometimes need to

00:55:30.000 --> 00:55:41.000
go outside of what you're ordinarily thinking about, and so these diagrams help inspire people to think about many different things

00:55:41.000 --> 00:55:44.000
Finally

00:55:44.000 --> 00:55:49.000
I'll talk about Shogyo Singo, I'm sorry

00:55:49.000 --> 00:55:57.000
So Shingo was a Japanese industrial engineer who really adopted this idea of total quality management

00:55:57.000 --> 00:56:02.000
And he is the major contributor to what's still now used

00:56:02.000 --> 00:56:05.000
Is the Toyota production system

00:56:05.000 --> 00:56:12.000
And it's used by companies worldwide, not only for cars, but other things as well

00:56:12.000 --> 00:56:18.000
He also was a big advocate of just-in-time production

00:56:18.000 --> 00:56:31.000
Which is not to build a lot of things in advance and store them to have them ready in inventory. But to try to build things just in time, just at the time that they needed to be

00:56:31.000 --> 00:56:34.000
produced for the customer

00:56:34.000 --> 00:56:46.000
And because that made the process much more efficient, it didn't mean… it meant you didn't have to store a lot of inventory, either on the part side or on the product side

00:56:46.000 --> 00:56:55.000
So how do you… how do you work it so that you get things just in time? And that goes into the idea of lean production, right?

00:56:55.000 --> 00:57:05.000
to do as little as possible, spend as little effort as possible to produce that, not to have extra labor, extra time, extra parts

00:57:05.000 --> 00:57:11.000
To try to be lean in producing your product or service

00:57:11.000 --> 00:57:15.000
And anyway, she's very important for that.

00:57:15.000 --> 00:57:17.000
And then

00:57:17.000 --> 00:57:25.000
Genichi Taguchi was also very important. He was a Japanese engineer and statistician

00:57:25.000 --> 00:57:30.000
And he's most famous for the Taguchi loss function

00:57:30.000 --> 00:57:35.000
And it measures financial loss to society from poor quality

00:57:35.000 --> 00:57:42.000
And I'll tell you, we'll talk about another person that has really built this out

00:57:42.000 --> 00:57:48.000
And extended this idea a lot as Dr. Tom Redmond

00:57:48.000 --> 00:57:52.000
But the idea that you

00:57:52.000 --> 00:57:56.000
You don't just think about

00:57:56.000 --> 00:58:00.000
You know, the immediate consequence

00:58:00.000 --> 00:58:04.000
of poor quality. In other words, you've got

00:58:04.000 --> 00:58:15.000
Poor quality and give you a great example, a real example that happened not too long ago up in Vermont, I believe

00:58:15.000 --> 00:58:18.000
And that's the idea that

00:58:18.000 --> 00:58:23.000
you know, in the county assessor's office is

00:58:23.000 --> 00:58:30.000
They assess the values of property, usually houses and lots and things like that

00:58:30.000 --> 00:58:32.000
And

00:58:32.000 --> 00:58:47.000
It has… they collect a lot of information about, you know how large is the house, how many square feet and so forth that they can use to sort of get an idea of how much the value would be and then how much tax to charge

00:58:47.000 --> 00:58:53.000
Or how to apply the tax rates to that value of the property.

00:58:53.000 --> 00:59:01.000
But often this information is shared with real estate people because they need to be able to sort of estimate the cost of

00:59:01.000 --> 00:59:18.000
houses or the prices of houses somebody's selling, they'll say, well, what about the houses that were nearby? Were they similar? Do they have similar square footage, and so forth, and what did they sell for? So, real estate people use that. And so, in this little town in Vermont

00:59:18.000 --> 00:59:25.000
One of the real estate agents was allowed to come in and directly access data

00:59:25.000 --> 00:59:30.000
from the tax assessor's office

00:59:30.000 --> 00:59:33.000
But she made a mistake

00:59:33.000 --> 00:59:38.000
And the way this was a system wasn't very sophisticated

00:59:38.000 --> 00:59:43.000
computer system they were using, but the upshot was

00:59:43.000 --> 00:59:54.000
She actually, in looking at the data, changed the value of one of the houses that she was looking at, and to a really, you know, I mean

00:59:54.000 --> 01:00:07.000
really big. So it was a house that was only a few hundred thousand dollars and suddenly it became worth a few million dollars just because she injected this error into the system

01:00:07.000 --> 01:00:17.000
Well, of course, when they went to produce the tax bills, one of the tax bills came out for this House that was a few hundred thousand dollars

01:00:17.000 --> 01:00:25.000
And then all of a sudden, the taxes were based on several million dollars. And of course, the guy that owned the house came in and said, look.

01:00:25.000 --> 01:00:29.000
This can't be right. And yeah, they went in and they fixed it

01:00:29.000 --> 01:00:45.000
So the point is that Toguchi's loss function says, well, it's not just that, it's not just that it produced one bad text bill, because what also happened was that between the time that the error was made and the error was discovered

01:00:45.000 --> 01:00:54.000
They had already gone out and used the values in the system to project the budgets

01:00:54.000 --> 01:01:02.000
for the next year for the county. And you know what are they going to do? What roads were they going to repair and so forth

01:01:02.000 --> 01:01:17.000
So when they produced that budget, it was much bigger than the previous year, but it wasn't so big that they said, oh, something's wrong, so they just said, hey, man, you know, we've got a lot of extra money this year, let's use it. And they went and they made commitments to contractors

01:01:17.000 --> 01:01:27.000
And undertook new projects and already had that underway before they found out that there was an error in the system

01:01:27.000 --> 01:01:45.000
And so even though you could say, well, when you look at the loss function, well, it wasn't much loss to go fix that one tax bill and make that consumer happy. That was a small… but if you look at the bigger impact of all of that

01:01:45.000 --> 01:02:00.000
It caused a lot of problems because they had already let contracts out for this extra money, and they had to pull those contracts back, had to pay penalties. People thought they were going to get employed, got let go because

01:02:00.000 --> 01:02:05.000
things didn't go through. So, Taguchi's loss function tries to measure

01:02:05.000 --> 01:02:14.000
All of the laws that's caused by when these problems happen, not just the immediate

01:02:14.000 --> 01:02:18.000
And he talked about offline quality control

01:02:18.000 --> 01:02:33.000
products and processes and sensitive to parameters outside the design engineer's control. In other words, again, this more broad thinking, not only the overall consequences of poor quality, but what are the things

01:02:33.000 --> 01:02:48.000
That you need to think about that oftentimes design engineers don't really think about when they're thinking more about how to efficiently build something and they're not necessarily thinking about

01:02:48.000 --> 01:02:53.000
These parameters that are outside the engineer's control

01:02:53.000 --> 01:03:01.000
So having broader thinking, bringing more people into the study process

01:03:01.000 --> 01:03:11.000
And then this idea that now has become enshrined in the idea of digital twins, but a lot of this developed before we had those capabilities

01:03:11.000 --> 01:03:18.000
And that was the idea of trying to design things statistically

01:03:18.000 --> 01:03:27.000
through experiments before you actually commit to building a final design of a product

01:03:27.000 --> 01:03:32.000
So let me quickly run through some of the

01:03:32.000 --> 01:03:35.000
More contemporary

01:03:35.000 --> 01:03:42.000
And I guess I said Larry English, who was the founder of Information Impact International and author

01:03:42.000 --> 01:03:50.000
He was responsible for this POSMAD that we talked about, this non-linear life cycle, this idea that

01:03:50.000 --> 01:04:03.000
You know, data has a life cycle in which you plan for it, obtain it, but then once you have it and store it and share it, you continuously apply it and maintain it

01:04:03.000 --> 01:04:06.000
And then, at some point dispose of it

01:04:06.000 --> 01:04:10.000
And he's best known for his books on

01:04:10.000 --> 01:04:16.000
The data warehouse is where this all came from. He has two very important books

01:04:16.000 --> 01:04:19.000
Information quality applied

01:04:19.000 --> 01:04:25.000
And improving data warehouse and business information quality.

01:04:25.000 --> 01:04:26.000
He's a great fellow

01:04:26.000 --> 01:04:34.000
And then Dr. Wong, who now is the director of the MIT Chief Data Officer and Information Quality Program

01:04:34.000 --> 01:04:36.000
He's also

01:04:36.000 --> 01:04:39.000
was he just retired as

01:04:39.000 --> 01:04:43.000
a professor here of information science

01:04:43.000 --> 01:04:58.000
And his major contribution was adapting this idea of total quality management and manufacturing to data quality in what he called total data quality management

01:04:58.000 --> 01:05:03.000
And he established the International Conference on Information Quality

01:05:03.000 --> 01:05:05.000
And

01:05:05.000 --> 01:05:13.000
instrumental in establishing information qualities and information as an academic discipline. Here, he's one of the founders here

01:05:13.000 --> 01:05:22.000
And he was the executive director for the Institute for Chief Data Officers here

01:05:22.000 --> 01:05:30.000
Another is very well known, and we'll be studying this over and over in this course, is the Wongstrong framework for Data Quality and Dimensions

01:05:30.000 --> 01:05:34.000
16 dimensions we talked about

01:05:34.000 --> 01:05:40.000
And sort of emphasizing what would already been done in manufacturing

01:05:40.000 --> 01:05:50.000
And the idea of managing information as product. In other words, focusing on the product side, and then working backwards, or working outwards both ways from that.

01:05:50.000 --> 01:05:58.000
And he's probably the most cited author in information quality research

01:05:58.000 --> 01:06:03.000
And currently is really advocating for this idea of chief state officer

01:06:03.000 --> 01:06:11.000
Several books, Journey to Data Quality, Quality Information and Knowledge, Introduction to Information Quality

01:06:11.000 --> 01:06:14.000
and data quality

01:06:14.000 --> 01:06:20.000
Another is Dr. Tom Redmond, who likes to call himself the Data Doc

01:06:20.000 --> 01:06:29.000
And again, going along the lines that we talked about a while ago of Taguchi's loss function

01:06:29.000 --> 01:06:34.000
The understanding of the cost of poor debt, that's one of his things

01:06:34.000 --> 01:06:41.000
And he developed this idea of rule of 10. It costs 10 times as much to complete a unit of work

01:06:41.000 --> 01:06:46.000
When the input data are defective as it does when the input data are perfect

01:06:46.000 --> 01:06:52.000
In other words, data quality really slows down by order of magnitude the process

01:06:52.000 --> 01:06:57.000
And this idea of information change, how information moves

01:06:57.000 --> 01:07:00.000
along in

01:07:00.000 --> 01:07:09.000
through a process, and how oftentimes this is sort of opposite to the way the company thinks about things.

01:07:09.000 --> 01:07:14.000
So data passes from department to department

01:07:14.000 --> 01:07:31.000
And we don't have… and people understand the data when it gets to them and what they need to do with it, but we don't have many organizations who don't have a good understanding of how that data flows and the overall flow of the data

01:07:31.000 --> 01:07:36.000
How it affects the organization and is affected by the organization.

01:07:36.000 --> 01:07:41.000
And he's written several books. I need to bring this up today. Getting in Front on Data

